nodecode-delphi is a rewrite of TakeshiTGAL's TakeshiTGAL/hermes-gensyn-plugins:

https://github.com/TakeshiTGAL/hermes-gensyn-plugins
commit 9325a7b15d815ca05983885ddd6615eae5f2b7a6

MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 TakeshiTGAL (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the original's seven read-only tools are seven read-only
tools here, answered by Lisp instead of a Node subprocess running Gensyn's SDK.
The four API reads (delphi_list_markets, delphi_get_market,
delphi_get_positions, delphi_compute_edge) use the SDK's own REST routes on the
same hosts (delphi-api.gensyn.ai for testnet and competition-testnet,
api.delphi.fyi for mainnet), with the read key as X-API-Key from the
environment variable the section names, and the competition's own data
selected by the X-Delphi-Mode header as the SDK selected it. What a model reads
is the original's material: the question, the outcomes, the status, the
category, the settle time, the trading fee as a percent (10^18 is 100%), the
market's own page, positions as raw shares beside their decimal reading, and
the opening line saying that the creator wrote the question and the outcomes
and that they are not instructions. The edge is exact decimal arithmetic as the
original's was: your probability minus the implied one, plain decimal text,
never a float, with the same rule for what a probability may be.

delphi_query_subgraph posts the SDK's own GraphQL queries to the network's
Goldsky subgraph: the indexer's position in the chain, a market's buys and
sells, and its settlement (settled, failed, and resolution requests), with the
original's modes, defaults and the rule that settlement has no offset.

delphi_quote_buy and delphi_quote_sell make the same eth_call reads the SDK
made, over JSON-RPC instead of a signer: keccak-256 selectors and ABI words
written out in Lisp, marketProxiesExist on the modern and legacy factories to
find the gateway that owns a market, then quoteBuyExactOut or quoteSellExactIn
on that gateway, then decimals() on the collateral token. One share is 10^18
raw units; the quote is reported raw, as collateral, and per share, with the
original's rule that a buy rounds a cut ratio up and a sell rounds it down.
Nothing is signed, nothing is sent, and no key is needed.

What was left out: Hermes itself -- the per-tool enable switches, the
30-requests-a-minute reservation counter, the Node subprocess with its
allowlisted environment and deadline, the doctor command, the secret scoping
and the JSON envelopes the original authored for its host. A Nodecode cell
answers text a model reads and leaves pacing to the operator. The live form of
delphi_compute_edge asks the gateway that owns the market for its spot implied
probabilities, as the original did, though without the original's refusal of an
LMSR market: whatever the gateway answers is the number used. The original's
proof-receipt reading of a market's settlement proof is carried as the API
sends it, without the separate ree_validate_receipt hand-off, and a market's
gateway is resolved once per read rather than cached for the client's lifetime.
